;;; A wildcard `_` as a pattern's relation head stops the run with a Python
;;; spelling — M1e2 **W20**, register row `W20/wild-in-head`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1e2.2.20 (2026-11-05) from the
;;; user's ruling of 2026-11-04 (Q-M1e2.13 (a)).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds a)`, and one rule that concludes `(hit w)` from any
;;; fact whose one argument is `a`, whatever its relation: the pattern's
;;; head is the wildcard `_`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `_` in a rule's pattern matches any value, in the head and in an
;;; argument, so `(_ a)` matches every fact of one argument `a`: `(holds a)`
;;; at root, and the candidate `(hit a)` where it is committed. Root holds
;;; `(hit w)`, and R is `{(hit w)}` and `{(hit a), (hit w)}`. It is the
;;; answer of `wild-in-head-twin.ein`, whose `:match` is the disjunction over
;;; the relations this program has that a one-argument fact can belong to,
;;; `holds` and `hit` (the declarations' own one-argument `relation` facts
;;; name `holds` and `hit`, never `a`). A head written as a fresh variable is
;;; no twin: M1 binds a relation head by the activator (Q29), so `(?r a)`
;;; with no parameter `?r` stops compilation as `_` does.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Every command that compiles the rule exits 1: `ein saturate` with
;;; `ein.inference.compile.CompileError: unbound relation head ?Wildcard() in
;;; a premise of `SForm(head=Wildcard(), args=(Atom(name='a'),))` — M1
;;; matches relations per activator (Q29), …`, and `ein solve` and `ein
;;; oracle` with the message alone, the Python IR's spelling of the pattern
;;; (`ein solve FILE` at `c73a0796`, release and debug).

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds a)
(rule w () :match (_ a) :assert (hit w))
